# Spin the Dial — deploy config.
#
# Your Spotify app's Client ID. It is public by design: PKCE uses no client secret,
# so there is nothing to hide and no backend or token proxy is needed. Everyone who
# opens the app signs in with their OWN Spotify account.
#
# Copy this file to .env.local for local development, or set the variable in your
# host's dashboard (Vercel / Netlify / etc.) for a shared deploy.
#
# Leave it unset and the app falls back to asking each visitor to paste an ID.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID=

# Last.fm API key, for the second way in: a public Last.fm username, read straight from the
# browser. Create one at https://www.last.fm/api/account/create
#
# Public by design, exactly like the Spotify Client ID. Last.fm also issues a *shared secret*
# alongside it — this app does not use or want it. The secret is only needed for private
# profiles and scrobbling, which require signing requests on a server. Leave it out.
#
# Unset means the Last.fm option is simply not offered on the setup screen.
NEXT_PUBLIC_LASTFM_API_KEY=

# Public URL of the deploy, used for the canonical link and absolute social-card URLs.
# Optional on Vercel: it falls back to VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL / VERCEL_URL, which
# Vercel injects for you. Set it once a custom domain is in front, and set it on any other
# host — otherwise these URLs point at 127.0.0.1, which no share preview can fetch.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=
